186+85	    /// Reads `SIRIUS_CN_ASYNC_SENDER_DISPATCH` once at bring-up: `1`, `true` or `on` queue
187+85	    /// sender-only fragments (no exchange input, no RESULT_SINK) on the dispatch worker instead of
188+85	    /// running them inside their exec_plan_fragment RPC. Off by default.
189+85	    ///
190+85	    /// Why it exists: the FE deploys the first fragment instance of every node in one wave and
191+85	    /// waits for all of those RPCs before it deploys the remaining instances. A sender that runs
192+85	    /// inside its RPC holds that wave open for its whole scan, so on a gather (q06: partial ->
193+85	    /// EXCHANGE UNPARTITIONED) the node that also hosts the merge fragment only receives its own
194+85	    /// scan after every other node's scan has finished. Measured on 4 GB200 CNs at SF1000: three
195+85	    /// scans started together and the fourth 726 ms later, right after the last `transmitted
196+85	    /// batches via nixl`. Returning as soon as the fragment is queued lets every scan start in the
197+85	    /// same wave.
