199+85	    /// Why it is off by default: a queued sender's failure (a malformed sink, an untranslatable
200+85	    /// plan, a failed remote drain) is attributed through `results.fail_query`, which only reaches
201+85	    /// result instances reserved on this node. When the result fragment lives on another node the
202+85	    /// FE learns of the failure through its query timeout instead of this RPC's status. Flip the
203+85	    /// default once a sender failure is forwarded to the receiver's node.
204+85	    fn async_sender_dispatch_from_env() -> bool {
